<header><p class="st-kicker">prince bishopric · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Imperial Abbey of Herford</h1><p class="st-meta">789 CE – 1803 CE (approx.) · capital Herford</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A convent for noble women in Westphalia whose abbesses ruled as princesses of the Empire for a thousand years.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herford">Herford</a> Abbey was founded around 789 by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> noble named Waltger as a community of women, soon after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne's</a> conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-pious">Louis the Pious</a> it became a royal abbey, and it was among the oldest women's foundations in Saxony. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matilda-of-ringelheim">Queen Matilda</a>, wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-fowler">Henry the Fowler</a>, was educated there.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herford">Herford</a> was a secular canonesses' foundation for daughters of the high nobility. Its abbess was a princess of the Empire with a seat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-diet">Imperial Diet</a>. The town of Herford grew around the abbey and joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a>. In the 17th century Princess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elisabeth-of-the-palatinate">Elisabeth of the Palatinate</a>, correspondent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rene-descartes">Descartes</a>, was abbess.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The town and abbey accepted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>, the abbey becoming Lutheran, but its princely status remained under the protection of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg-prussia">Brandenburg-Prussia</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herford">Herford's</a> imperial status ended with secularisation in 1803, when its lands passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>. The convent was formally dissolved in 1810.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/imperial-abbey-of-herford.jpg" alt="Imperial Abbey of Herford" width="320" height="214"><figcaption>Image: &quot;Ingo Kirschnereit Cyclejakob&quot; · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Muensterkirche-frontview.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>789 CE – 1803 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>prince bishopric</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Herford</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Elisabeth of the Palatinate</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity, later Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Low German, Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Herford Abbey</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1296&amp;focus=imperial-abbey-of-herford" aria-label="Imperial Abbey of Herford on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4875 377 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Imperial Abbey of Herford lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5195 633L5229 694M5195 633L5300 622"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5300 622h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5195 633h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5195 633h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1296&amp;focus=imperial-abbey-of-herford">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
